+3.1 Suppose there is a compressing annulus $A$. First arrange its end curves without imposing any additional assumption on the Statement's word "on." A finite PL boundary collar of $M$ can be chosen compatible with the proper surface $S$, so $S$ in it is $(\partial S)\times[0,a]$. Here is the required local verification. In a boundary vertex link, the link of $S$ is a proper polygonal arc in the ambient half-link disk. In a PL parameter of the link disk, adjoin each of the two boundary subarcs to this proper arc. The two resulting simple polygons bound the two sides of the arc by the polygonal separation in [F2], and their PL disk parameters agree along that arc after prescribing its boundary map. Mapping these disks to the two sides of a standard diameter, with the same prescribed endpoint maps, straightens the arc. Coning the link map gives a simultaneous half-space/surface product chart. The finite collar construction in [F3] can then be performed in these compatible charts: subdivide with the marked arc a subcomplex and take its local cone-vertex pushes in the marked product strata. Each push preserves the marked surface, so their finite composite gives the claimed compatible collar. If $\gamma$ meets $\partial S$, choose a PL function $\chi$ on $\partial M$, positive near that compact intersection and zero near the disjoint other boundary circle of $A$. On a smaller collar replace each vertical interval $[0,a]$ by $[\eta\chi(x),a]$, with $0\le\eta\chi<a$, fixing its top. This gives a PL inward embedding: on each base simplex the target is the convex prism between the affine graph $\eta\chi$ and height $a$; prescribe compatible boundary maps and cone matching boundary triangulations from interior points with the same base coordinate. The affine extensions preserve projection and are bijective. Extend by the identity above the collar. It carries $S$ into $S$, off-$S$ points to off-$S$ points, moves $\gamma$ into $\operatorname{int}S$, and fixes the other annulus end. Shrinking the graph height continuously to zero gives a homotopy to inclusion and an isotopy of the inner circle in $S$, so the core injection, essentiality and non-boundary-parallel condition are retained. This is an inward embedding, not an onto ambient isotopy. Extend the collar height by the constant $a$ outside the collar. Restricted to the resulting finite PL annulus, this PL function is zero exactly on its outer boundary. Triangulate so this height is affine. For a positive $\delta$ below all positive vertex heights and below the height of the inner end, its sublevel consists of the finite boundary fans along that one zero boundary circle: each edge strip meets the $\delta$-level in one arc, and the vertex fans join adjacent arcs once in their cyclic order. Thus the level is one embedded circle parallel to the outer end. It lies in the single ambient collar level $\delta$, whose projection to $\partial M$ is a homeomorphism, so its projected circle is embedded. The projection misses $\partial S$, because the level circle misses $S$ and the collar is compatible with $S$. Truncate $A$ at this circle and append its vertical annulus down to $\partial M$. The new annulus is embedded and misses $S$ in its interior, since its retained part has height at least $\delta$ and its appended part has height at most $\delta$ on fibers outside $\partial S$. The replacement is homotopic on the core through the discarded end collar, hence preserves the injected core class. Its inner end is in $\operatorname{int}S$ and its outer end is now neat and disjoint from $S$. [F2, F3, step 2.1, construct, algebra]
 
-3.1 If $\gamma$ separates a negative-Euler component, neither resulting component is a disk because $\gamma$ is essential, and neither is an annulus because that would make $\gamma$ parallel to an original boundary component. Both resulting components therefore have negative Euler characteristic. Their total genus and negative Euler characteristic equal the original totals, while their number of boundary circles increases by two. Hence $D,G$ are unchanged and $q=3D-B_-$ decreases by two. If $\gamma$ is nonseparating, its connected cut surface has the same Euler characteristic, genus one less and two more boundary circles, so $D$ is unchanged and $G$ decreases. The only zero-Euler cases are tori and annuli. An essential curve on a torus is nonseparating, so the torus becomes an annulus and $G$ decreases. On an annulus every essential embedded circle is boundary-parallel, which is excluded in the definition of a compressing annulus: cap its two ends by disks to obtain a sphere and apply [F2] to see that an essential circle separates the two caps and cobounds an annulus with each original boundary. These cases exhaust [F2] and each contradicts lexicographic minimality in step 1.1. Therefore no compressing annulus exists. [F2, step 1.1, step 2.2, algebra]
